{
  "schema": "grv-cafe-reflections/1",
  "updated": "2026-10-18",
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "rf-01",
      "text": "Every day is a fresh beginning, every morn is the world made new.",
      "by": "Susan Coolidge",
      "source": {
        "work": "New Every Morning (A Few More Verses)",
        "year": 1889,
        "url": "https://gutenberg.org/files/49518/49518-h/49518-h.htm"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-02",
      "text": "For in the dew of little things the heart finds its morning and is refreshed.",
      "by": "Kahlil Gibran",
      "source": {
        "work": "The Prophet, On Friendship",
        "year": 1923,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/58585/pg58585-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-03",
      "text": "Hope is the thing with feathers that perches in the soul.",
      "by": "Emily Dickinson",
      "source": {
        "work": "Poems, Second Series",
        "year": 1891,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12242/pg12242-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-04",
      "text": "What is this life if, full of care, we have no time to stand and stare.",
      "by": "W. H. Davies",
      "source": {
        "work": "Leisure (Songs of Joy and Others)",
        "year": 1911,
        "url": "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Collected_Poems_of_William_H._Davies/Leisure"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-05",
      "text": "What do we live for, if it is not to make life less difficult to each other?",
      "by": "George Eliot",
      "source": {
        "work": "Middlemarch, chapter 72",
        "year": 1872,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/145"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-07",
      "text": "My heart is like an apple-tree whose boughs are bent with thickset fruit.",
      "by": "Christina Rossetti",
      "source": {
        "work": "A Birthday (Goblin Market and Other Poems)",
        "year": 1862,
        "url": "https://rpo.library.utoronto.ca/poems/birthday"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-08",
      "text": "When thou wishest to delight thyself, think of the virtues of those who live with thee.",
      "by": "Marcus Aurelius",
      "source": {
        "work": "Meditations, book VI (George Long translation)",
        "year": 1862,
        "url": "https://classics.mit.edu/Antoninus/meditations.6.six.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-09",
      "text": "If Winter comes, can Spring be far behind?",
      "by": "Percy Bysshe Shelley",
      "source": {
        "work": "Ode to the West Wind",
        "year": 1820,
        "url": "https://poets.org/poem/ode-west-wind"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-10",
      "text": "The butterfly counts not months but moments, and has time enough.",
      "by": "Rabindranath Tagore",
      "source": {
        "work": "Fireflies",
        "year": 1928,
        "url": "https://archive.org/details/dli.venugopal.844"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-11",
      "text": "The only reward of virtue is virtue; the only way to have a friend is to be one.",
      "by": "Ralph Waldo Emerson",
      "source": {
        "work": "Friendship (Essays: First Series)",
        "year": 1841,
        "url": "https://emersoncentral.com/texts/essays-first-series/friendship/"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-12",
      "text": "The journey of a thousand li commenced with a single step.",
      "by": "Lao Tzu",
      "source": {
        "work": "Tao Te Ching, chapter 64 (James Legge translation)",
        "year": 1891,
        "url": "https://gutenberg.org/cache/epub/216/pg216.txt"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-13",
      "text": "Isn’t it nice to think that tomorrow is a new day with no mistakes in it yet?",
      "by": "L. M. Montgomery",
      "source": {
        "work": "Anne of Green Gables, chapter 21",
        "year": 1908,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45/pg45-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-14",
      "text": "Nature’s peace will flow into you as sunshine flows into trees.",
      "by": "John Muir",
      "source": {
        "work": "Our National Parks",
        "year": 1901,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/60929/pg60929-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-15",
      "text": "How far that little candle throws his beams! So shines a good deed in a naughty world.",
      "by": "William Shakespeare",
      "source": {
        "work": "The Merchant of Venice, act 5",
        "year": 1600,
        "url": "https://shakespeare.mit.edu/merchant/merchant.5.1.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-16",
      "text": "Very little indeed is necessary for living a happy life.",
      "by": "Marcus Aurelius",
      "source": {
        "work": "Meditations, book VII (George Long translation)",
        "year": 1862,
        "url": "https://classics.mit.edu/Antoninus/meditations.7.seven.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-17",
      "text": "The world is so full of a number of things, I’m sure we should all be as happy as kings.",
      "by": "Robert Louis Stevenson",
      "source": {
        "work": "Happy Thought (A Child’s Garden of Verses)",
        "year": 1885,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/136/pg136-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-32",
      "text": "Where you tend a rose, my lad, a thistle cannot grow.",
      "by": "Frances Hodgson Burnett",
      "source": {
        "work": "The Secret Garden, chapter 27",
        "year": 1911,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/113"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-19",
      "text": "And then my heart with pleasure fills, and dances with the Daffodils.",
      "by": "William Wordsworth",
      "source": {
        "work": "I Wandered Lonely as a Cloud (Poems, in Two Volumes)",
        "year": 1807,
        "url": "https://romantic-circles.org/sites/default/files/RCOldSite/www/rchs/reader/wwdaff.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-20",
      "text": "Is it not delightful to have friends coming from distant quarters?",
      "by": "Confucius",
      "source": {
        "work": "The Analects, book I (James Legge translation)",
        "year": 1861,
        "url": "https://gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3330/pg3330-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-21",
      "text": "Sit still my heart, do not raise your dust. Let the world find its way to you.",
      "by": "Rabindranath Tagore",
      "source": {
        "work": "Stray Birds",
        "year": 1916,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6524/pg6524-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-22",
      "text": "I’m not afraid of storms, for I’m learning how to sail my ship.",
      "by": "Louisa May Alcott",
      "source": {
        "work": "Little Women, Part Two, chapter 44",
        "year": 1869,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/514/pg514-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-23",
      "text": "Write it on your heart that every day is the best day in the year.",
      "by": "Ralph Waldo Emerson",
      "source": {
        "work": "Works and Days (Society and Solitude)",
        "year": 1870,
        "url": "https://emersoncentral.com/texts/society-and-solitude/works-and-days/"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-24",
      "text": "Little friends may prove great friends.",
      "by": "Aesop",
      "source": {
        "work": "The Lion and the Mouse (retold by Joseph Jacobs, The Fables of Aesop)",
        "year": 1894,
        "url": "https://gutenberg.org/cache/epub/28/pg28-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-25",
      "text": "If I can stop one heart from breaking, I shall not live in vain.",
      "by": "Emily Dickinson",
      "source": {
        "work": "Poems, First Series",
        "year": 1890,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12242/pg12242-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-26",
      "text": "There is nothing—absolute nothing—half so much worth doing as simply messing about in boats.",
      "by": "Kenneth Grahame",
      "source": {
        "work": "The Wind in the Willows, chapter 1",
        "year": 1908,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/289/pg289-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-27",
      "text": "Hope springs eternal in the human breast.",
      "by": "Alexander Pope",
      "source": {
        "work": "An Essay on Man, Epistle I",
        "year": 1733,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2428/pg2428-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-28",
      "text": "And forget not that the earth delights to feel your bare feet and the winds long to play with your hair.",
      "by": "Kahlil Gibran",
      "source": {
        "work": "The Prophet, On Clothes",
        "year": 1923,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/58585/pg58585-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-29",
      "text": "No one is useless in this world who lightens the burden of it for any one else.",
      "by": "Charles Dickens",
      "source": {
        "work": "Our Mutual Friend",
        "year": 1865,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/883"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-30",
      "text": "I would always rather be happy than dignified.",
      "by": "Charlotte Brontë",
      "source": {
        "work": "Jane Eyre, chapter 34",
        "year": 1847,
        "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1260/pg1260-images.html"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "rf-31",
      "text": "Only that day dawns to which we are awake. There is more day to dawn. The sun is but a morning star.",
      "by": "Henry David Thoreau",
      "source": {
        "work": "Walden, Conclusion",
        "year": 1854,
        "url": "https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Walden,_or,_Life_in_the_Woods/Conclusion"
      }
    }
  ]
}
